  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nEarly this Morning:\n\nA strong shortwave dives southeast overnight with an occluded front \npressing in from the west. Temperatures aloft are expected to rapidly \ncool as an upper level low churns across Manitoba into Ontario.\nThis will lead to steep mid level lapse rate to further aide in\nstorm growth. Timing and placement of storms remain contested \namong the high res guidance but they are beginning to hone in \nthe main activity being after midnight. The convective parameter\nspace will boast MUCAPE over 800 J/kg and a strong upper level \njet will be nudging in across the Northern Plains to increase \nbulk shear values of 40-50 kts. This would promote the growth of\nelevated storms capable of large hail and some damaging winds. \nSPC has this largely outlined as a marginal threat (1/5) with a \nsmall sliver along our western counties of slight risk (2/5). \n\nMonday:\n\nLingering storms from the overnight hours are expected to exit by \nlate morning. The key to Monday's forecast will be the placement of \nthe frontal boundary. This remains a highly contested feature among \nall available guidance with the CAMS suggesting it will be out of \nCWA by the afternoon. Not confident enough to buy into this solution \ngiven how poor these suite of models have been performing as of \nlate. It seems reasonable to stick with what SPC has outlined which \nis an Enhanced risk (3/5) through Price county and a Slight risk \n(2/5) trailing behind it through NW WI and into NE MN. With the \nmorning activity expected to clear out fairly quickly we should have \nample time to refill the ingredients for severe storms in the \nafternoon. MLCAPE will be high with values over 2500 J/kg paired \nwith bulk shear of 40-50 kts. This combo of ingredients would support \nsupercell growth and low level helicity could also induce a few \ntornadoes. Again, all of this is predicated on the placement of the \nfrontal boundary for the afternoon and evening hours.\n\nFarther north, an upper level low will be transitioning into an open \nwave with cyclonic flow aloft coupled with plenty of PVA. Wrap \naround moisture around this cyclone will spawn scattered showers \nacross northern MN with some isolated storms possible. Weaker \ninstability across this area should keep development of stronger \nstorms at bay. Additionally, strong westerly winds will be filtering \nin across the region tomorrow with gusts over 30 mph expected \nthrough the day. \n\nTuesday: \n\nAnother wind shift from out of the north is in store for the \nNorthland on Tuesday. A weak cold front will also drop from out of \nthe north with low chances (15-20%) of precipitation, may end up \nwith numerous areas seeing sprinkles given the dry air aloft. Wind \ngusts in excess of 30 mph will be possible and could reintroduce \nanother round of smoke engulfing the region. High res smoke guidance \nis not properly displaying this outcome at the moment due to how the \nmodels initialize with hotspot information. But Air Quality \nwill be something to keep an eye on for the Tuesday timeframe. \n\nMidweek:\n\nFairly mild forecast midweek through the end of the work week. \nTemperatures will be trending back up into the upper 70s and low \n80s. The northerly winds taper off on Wednesday as high pressure \nmoves across the Upper Midwest. Our next batch of precipitation \nchances (20-30%) arrive Thursday afternoon as some weak surface \nconvergence floats through the region.",
